1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,009
This sea slug looks like a leaf.

2
00:00:02,009 --> 00:00:03,809
And in a way, it acts like one.

3
00:00:04,317 --> 00:00:08,767
Elysia chlorotica eats a green alga, but doesn't fully digest it.

4
00:00:08,767 --> 00:00:13,013
It keeps the alga's chloroplasts working inside its own cells.

5
00:00:13,517 --> 00:00:16,430
Those stolen solar panels keep working for months.

6
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:19,402
The slug can go nine months or more without eating.

7
00:00:19,917 --> 00:00:23,207
Scientists once thought it had stolen algae genes too.

8
00:00:23,207 --> 00:00:25,827
Later sequencing found no evidence of that.

9
00:00:25,827 --> 00:00:29,056
How the chloroplasts keep running is still a mystery.

10
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:34,487
And some of its relatives can drop their own body and regrow a whole new one from the head.

11
00:00:34,983 --> 00:00:37,275
So could you eat sunlight like a sea slug?

12
00:00:37,275 --> 00:00:38,694
Full story on the channel.
